## Goal
Write the memo for the weekly platform meeting recorded in the notes file /private/tmp/hirearchy-g0-m0-s2-1792995184/notes/2026-10-19.md (read only that file; nothing from other meetings). Write it to lead/writer/work/memo-2026-10-19.md.

## Acceptance criteria
C1: Covers only the meeting in the requested notes file and nothing else.
C2: At most 400 words as counted by read.
C3: Has a section titled Decisions that lists every decision the notes record as made (decided or agreed), one line each, and no item that is not a decision.
C4: Has a section titled Actions with a markdown table whose columns are exactly Owner | Action | Due.
C5: The table has one row for every commitment in the notes, including informal ones stated in prose (for example someone saying they will look into something), not only items labelled ACTION.
C6: Due is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) when the notes give a date, or a deadline that resolves to one from the meeting date (a named weekday means that weekday of the meeting's week; the next meeting means the date the notes give for it). Due is 'none' when the notes give no date, say no hard date, or give only a loose period such as 'this week'.
C7: Every decision and every table row is traceable to the notes file; no owner, action or date is invented.
C11: The memo's first line is a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, with nothing before it (no title and no Source line), followed by the Decisions section, then the Actions table, in that order.

Format: markdown, at most 400 words, file named memo-2026-10-19.md.

Requester's note (verbatim): The memo's first line must be the one-line 'Summary:' with nothing before it (no title, no Source line); then Decisions; then the Actions table; a closing next-meeting line goes after the table. Client rulings from earlier memos: a named weekday or 'day before X' resolves to that date; a promise made in prose counts as an action even when hedged ('probably'); an item that is only an action is not also listed as a decision; no commentary sections; an item with no date in the notes gets Due none. List any real ambiguity as a question for the client rather than deciding it.

How to handle ambiguity: the memo itself has no commentary or Open questions section. If an item could reasonably be read two ways (e.g. is it a decision or only an offer; is it a commitment or not; does a loose phrase resolve to a date), apply the rulings above, and list each such item with the notes' exact words in your report to me (not in the memo) so I can put it to the client. Do not silently drop or add items.

